```cpp
class MyData {
    int* arr;
    size_t size;
public:
    // Assume MyData(size_t s) constructor exists
    MyData(MyData&& other) noexcept : arr(nullptr), size(0) {
        // 1. Steal the resources from 'other'
        arr = other.arr;
        size = other.size;
        // 2. Nullify 'other's pointers to prevent double-free
        other.arr = nullptr;
        other.size = 0;
    }
    // ... destructor, copy constructor, etc. ...
};
```
The move constructor takes an rvalue reference (`MyData&& other`), transfers ownership of `other`'s resources (`arr`, `size`) to the new object, and then sets `other` to a valid, empty state.

```cpp
template<typename T>
void log_and_process(T&& data) {
    std::cout << "Logging data...\n";
    // Call process_data, preserving data's original value category
    process_data(std::forward<T>(data));
}

// Example usage:
// void process_data(MyObject& obj); // for lvalues
// void process_data(MyObject&& obj); // for rvalues
```
`std::forward<T>(data)` ensures that if `data` was passed as an lvalue, it's forwarded as an lvalue reference; if it was passed as an rvalue, it's forwarded as an rvalue reference. This allows `process_data` to correctly invoke either its copy or move overload.
